<section xml:id="sec-mod4-study-guide">
  <title>Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <p>Reading: Zorn, Sections 2.1<ndash/>2.4. Estimated time: 60 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      This guide accompanies four sections of Zorn's <em>Understanding
      Real Analysis</em>, which together cover the full story of
      convergent sequences of real numbers. The weight of the module
      falls on &#xa7;2.1 (the definition of convergence) and &#xa7;2.3
      (the Monotone Convergence and Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass theorems);
      &#xa7;2.2 (limit laws) is labor-saving technology, and &#xa7;2.4
      (Cauchy sequences) reframes convergence in a form you will see
      again in MAT 5610.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read each section before working its questions. The questions
      are designed to be answered with the text open in front of you;
      quote definitions and theorem numbers rather than paraphrasing
      when you can.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 2.1: DEFINITION OF CONVERGENCE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-sg-21-intro">
    <title>Section 2.1: Sequences and Convergence (Zorn pp. 83<ndash/>95)</title>
    <p>
      This is the central section of the module. Read Zorn's Definition 2.1
      (convergence) more than once; understanding its quantifier
      structure is the main learning goal of Video 1 and of the whole
      module. Pay attention also to Zorn's Definition 2.2 (bounded,
      monotone) and Theorem 2.3 (a bounded monotone sequence converges).
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-21-1">
    <title>Parsing the Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write out Zorn's Definition 2.1 of convergence
        (<m>a_n \to L</m>) symbolically, using quantifiers
        (<m>\forall</m>, <m>\exists</m>) and the absolute value.
        Then answer:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            How many quantifiers appear, and in what order?
          </li>
          <li>
            Which variable is chosen by the adversary (i.e., you do
            not get to pick it), and which is chosen in response?
          </li>
          <li>
            After a proof of convergence is written, what is the
            natural functional relationship between the adversary's
            choice and the responder's choice: typically, does
            <m>N</m> get larger or smaller as <m>\varepsilon</m> gets
            smaller?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Three: <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0\ \exists N \in \N\
        \forall n \geq N: |a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon</m>. The order
        is <m>\forall</m>, <m>\exists</m>, <m>\forall</m>.
        (b) The adversary picks <m>\varepsilon</m>; you pick <m>N</m>
        in response. The final <m>\forall n \geq N</m> is a conclusion,
        not a choice.
        (c) <m>N</m> gets larger as <m>\varepsilon</m> gets smaller:
        a finer tolerance requires going farther out in the sequence.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-21-2">
    <title>Negating the Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write out the negation of convergence (that is, what
        <m>a_n \not\to L</m> means) by applying the standard
        negation rules from <xref ref="ch-mod1"/> (push <m>\neg</m> through each
        quantifier in turn).
      </p>
      <p>
        Then state what it means, in this same format, for the
        sequence <m>(a_n)</m> to be <term>divergent</term>, that is,
        for no <m>L \in \R</m> to be its limit.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>a_n \not\to L</m> means:
        <m>\exists \varepsilon_0 \gt 0\ \forall N \in \N\
        \exists n \geq N: |a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon_0</m>.
        Divergence is: <m>\forall L \in \R\ \exists \varepsilon_0 \gt 0\
        \forall N \in \N\ \exists n \geq N: |a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon_0</m>.
        The key point is that to disprove convergence to a specific
        <m>L</m>, you exhibit a single <m>\varepsilon_0</m> that fails
        for every <m>N</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-sg-two-facts">
    <title>Two Facts You Will Use Constantly</title>
    <p>
      Zorn's Definition 2.1 says what it means for <m>L</m> to be <em>a</em>
      limit of <m>(a_n)</m>. The first fact below justifies speaking of
      <em>the</em> limit and writing <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n</m>; the
      second lets you carry inequalities between terms over to their
      limits. Both follow directly from the definition of convergence
      (the first also uses the Triangle Inequality); neither uses
      completeness. Read the two proofs
      now, since the worked examples and later modules cite both facts.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod4-limit-unique">
      <title>Uniqueness of Limits</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>a_n \to L</m> and <m>a_n \to M</m>, then <m>L = M</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Suppose instead that <m>L \neq M</m>, and set
          <m>\varepsilon = |L - M|/2 \gt 0</m>. We choose <m>N_1</m> with
          <m>|a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all <m>n \geq N_1</m>, and
          <m>N_2</m> with <m>|a_n - M| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all
          <m>n \geq N_2</m>. For <m>n = \max(N_1, N_2)</m>, the Triangle
          Inequality gives
          <md>|L - M| \leq |L - a_n| + |a_n - M| \lt \varepsilon + \varepsilon = |L - M|,</md>
          which is impossible. Hence <m>L = M</m>. (You will compare this
          argument with Bauldry's in <xref ref="ex-mod4-br-2"/>.)
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod4-limit-order">
      <title>Limits Preserve Non-Strict Inequalities</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>a_n \to A</m>, <m>b_n \to B</m>, and
          <m>a_n \leq b_n</m> for all <m>n \geq N_0</m>, for some
          <m>N_0</m>, then
          <m>A \leq B</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Suppose instead that <m>A \gt B</m>, and set
          <m>\varepsilon = (A - B)/2 \gt 0</m>. We choose <m>N_1</m> with
          <m>|a_n - A| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all <m>n \geq N_1</m>, and
          <m>N_2</m> with <m>|b_n - B| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all
          <m>n \geq N_2</m>. For <m>n = \max(N_0, N_1, N_2)</m>,
          <md>b_n \lt B + \varepsilon = \frac{A + B}{2} = A - \varepsilon \lt a_n,</md>
          which contradicts <m>a_n \leq b_n</m>. Hence <m>A \leq B</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      The most common use takes one of the two sequences to be
      constant. The constant sequence <m>c, c, c, \ldots</m> converges
      to <m>c</m> (every term is at distance <m>0</m> from <m>c</m>), so
      if <m>a_n \to A</m> and <m>a_n \leq c</m> for all sufficiently
      large <m>n</m>, then <m>A \leq c</m>. Likewise, <m>a_n \geq c</m>
      for all sufficiently large <m>n</m> gives <m>A \geq c</m>.
      Strict inequalities need not survive the limit: <m>1/n \gt 0</m>
      for every <m>n</m>, yet <m>1/n \to 0</m>. From <m>a_n \lt b_n</m>
      you may conclude only <m>A \leq B</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-21-3">
    <title>From Scratch Work to Formal Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Zorn's worked examples in &#xa7;2.1, the author often first
        performs algebraic manipulation (finding the relationship
        between <m>n</m> and <m>\varepsilon</m>) and then writes a
        formal proof. Re-read Zorn's proof that <m>1/n \to 0</m>
        or, if &#xa7;2.1 does not give one, the first explicit
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> argument in &#xa7;2.1.
      </p>
      <p>
        Identify: where in the formal proof does the Archimedean
        Property appear, and why is it essential? Which of <xref ref="ch-mod2"/>'s
        tools is the Archimedean Property a consequence of?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-21-4">
    <title>Boundedness</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State Zorn's definition of a <em>bounded</em> sequence.
        Then prove Zorn's Theorem 2.4 informally:
        if <m>a_n \to L</m>, then <m>(a_n)</m> is bounded.
      </p>
      <p>
        (Sketch: apply the definition of convergence with
        <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> to bound the tail; then use the
        finiteness of the first <m>N</m> terms to bound the head.
        Write this as two or three complete sentences.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        A sequence is bounded if there exists <m>M \gt 0</m> such that
        <m>|a_n| \leq M</m> for all <m>n \in \N</m>. If <m>a_n \to L</m>,
        pick <m>N</m> so that <m>|a_n - L| \lt 1</m> for <m>n \geq N</m>;
        then <m>|a_n| \leq |L| + 1</m> for such <m>n</m>. The initial
        terms <m>a_1, \ldots, a_{N-1}</m> form a finite set, so
        <m>M = \max\{|a_1|, \ldots, |a_{N-1}|, |L| + 1\}</m> bounds
        the entire sequence. (If <m>N = 1</m>, there are no initial
        terms, and <m>M = |L| + 1</m>.)
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 2.2: ALGEBRA OF LIMITS AND SQUEEZE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-sg-22-intro">
    <title>Section 2.2: Working with Limits (Zorn pp. 96<ndash/>105)</title>
    <p>
      Section 2.2 collects the tools that let you evaluate limits of
      sums, products, quotients, and sandwiched sequences without
      returning to the definition. The main results are the Algebra of
      Limits (Zorn's Theorem 2.5) and the Squeeze Theorem (Zorn's Theorem 2.6).
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-22-1">
    <title>The Algebra of Limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>a_n \to A</m> and <m>b_n \to B</m>. State the
        three most commonly used conclusions from Zorn's Theorem 2.5
        (sum, product, quotient).
      </p>
      <p>
        For the quotient, what additional hypothesis is needed beyond
        <m>a_n \to A</m> and <m>b_n \to B</m>, and why is that
        hypothesis necessary?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Sum: <m>a_n + b_n \to A + B</m>. Product: <m>a_n b_n \to AB</m>.
        Quotient: <m>a_n / b_n \to A / B</m>, provided <m>B \neq 0</m>
        (and implicitly the <m>b_n</m> are eventually nonzero). The
        hypothesis <m>B \neq 0</m> is needed to avoid dividing by 0
        both in the limit and, via a tail argument, in the quotient
        <m>a_n/b_n</m> for large <m>n</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-22-2">
    <title>Reading the Proof of the Sum Rule</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's proof that <m>a_n + b_n \to A + B</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What algebraic identity launches the proof? (It is an
            application of the Triangle Inequality.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Why is the tolerance split as <m>\varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2</m>
            instead of simply <m>\varepsilon</m>?
          </li>
          <li>
            How is <m>N</m> chosen in terms of the <m>N_1</m> and
            <m>N_2</m> coming from <m>a_n \to A</m> and <m>b_n \to B</m>
            respectively?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>|(a_n + b_n) - (A + B)| \leq |a_n - A| + |b_n - B|</m>.
        (b) Each summand is bounded by <m>\varepsilon/2</m>, so their
        sum is bounded by <m>\varepsilon</m>, the desired bound.
        (c) <m>N = \max(N_1, N_2)</m>, so that both convergence bounds
        hold simultaneously for <m>n \geq N</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-22-3">
    <title>Using the Algebra of Limits on Rational Expressions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <md>\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{5n^2 - 3n}{2n^2 + n + 7}.</md>
        Do not write an <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> proof. Instead:
        divide numerator and denominator by <m>n^2</m>, identify each
        term's limit (using <m>1/n \to 0</m> and constants), and apply
        the Algebra of Limits to assemble the answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write your reasoning in complete sentences. Indicate
        each step at which the Algebra of Limits is applied.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Dividing gives <m>\dfrac{5 - 3/n}{2 + 1/n + 7/n^2}</m>.
        We know <m>1/n \to 0</m>, so
        <m>1/n^2 = (1/n)(1/n) \to 0 \cdot 0 = 0</m> by the product
        rule. The product rule with a constant sequence also gives <m>3/n = 3 \cdot (1/n) \to 0</m> and
        <m>7/n^2 = 7 \cdot (1/n^2) \to 0</m>. So we have numerator <m>\to 5 - 0 = 5</m>
        (sum rule) and denominator <m>\to 2 + 0 + 0 = 2</m>
        (sum rule, twice). Since the denominator's limit is
        nonzero, the quotient rule gives <m>5/2</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-22-4">
    <title>The Squeeze Theorem in Practice</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Squeeze Theorem from Zorn &#xa7;2.2 in your own
        words. Then apply it to show that <m>a_n = \dfrac{\cos(n^2)}{n}</m>
        converges, and identify the limit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your solution should explicitly write the two bounding
        sequences you are squeezing between, explain why each
        converges (to what), and then invoke the theorem.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-1 \leq \cos(n^2) \leq 1</m>, so <m>-1/n \leq a_n \leq 1/n</m>
        for all <m>n \in \N</m>. Both <m>-1/n \to 0</m> and
        <m>1/n \to 0</m> (by the Archimedean Property, or as a known limit),
        so by the Squeeze Theorem <m>a_n \to 0</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 2.3: MONOTONE AND BOLZANO-WEIERSTRASS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-sg-23-intro">
    <title>Section 2.3: Subsequences and the Big Theorems (Zorn pp. 106<ndash/>113)</title>
    <p>
      Section 2.3 introduces subsequences and proves two of the
      headline theorems of sequential analysis: the Monotone
      Convergence Theorem and the Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass Theorem.
      Both are consequences of completeness; each will be invoked
      repeatedly in MAT 5610.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-23-1">
    <title>Subsequences and Inheritance</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define <em>subsequence</em> precisely: what does it mean for
        <m>(a_{n_k})</m> to be a subsequence of <m>(a_n)</m>? What is
        required of the index sequence <m>n_1, n_2, \ldots</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        State (without proof) the inheritance result: if <m>a_n \to L</m>,
        what is the limit of every subsequence of <m>(a_n)</m>?
        State the contrapositive of the inheritance result and explain
        how it is used to <em>prove divergence</em>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>(a_{n_k})_{k=1}^{\infty}</m> is a subsequence of <m>(a_n)</m>
        if <m>n_1 \lt n_2 \lt n_3 \lt \cdots</m> is a strictly increasing
        sequence in <m>\N</m>. Inheritance: if <m>a_n \to L</m>, every
        subsequence also converges to <m>L</m>. Contrapositive: if
        <m>(a_n)</m> has two subsequences converging to different
        limits, then <m>(a_n)</m> diverges. This is the standard tool
        for showing that, e.g., <m>((-1)^n)</m> diverges: the even
        terms converge to 1 and the odd terms to <m>-1</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-23-2">
    <title>Where Completeness Enters the Monotone Convergence Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Monotone Convergence Theorem as Zorn presents it.
        Then re-read the proof slowly.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What set <m>S</m> does the proof consider, and why is
            <m>S</m> nonempty and bounded above?
          </li>
          <li>
            At what step is the Completeness Axiom invoked, and
            what object does it produce?
          </li>
          <li>
            How is the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization of the
            supremum (from <xref ref="ch-mod2"/>) used to produce the index
            <m>N</m> needed by the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m>
            definition of convergence?
          </li>
          <li>
            Where in the proof is the hypothesis that <m>(a_n)</m>
            is monotone actually used?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) For an increasing sequence (the decreasing case is
        symmetric, using the infimum),
        <m>S = \{a_n : n \in \N\}</m>, the set of terms. It is
        nonempty (contains <m>a_1</m>) and bounded above by the given
        bound on <m>(a_n)</m>.
        (b) Completeness is used to assert <m>L = \sup S \in \R</m>.
        (c) The <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization provides, for any
        <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, an index <m>N</m> with
        <m>a_N \gt L - \varepsilon</m>.
        (d) Monotonicity (increasingness) is used to pass from
        <m>a_N \gt L - \varepsilon</m> to <m>a_n \gt L - \varepsilon</m>
        for all <m>n \geq N</m>, because it guarantees that later terms do
        not drop back below <m>a_N</m>. Since <m>a_n \leq L</m> as well
        (<m>L</m> is an upper bound of <m>S</m>), this gives
        <m>L - \varepsilon \lt a_n \leq L</m>, so
        <m>|a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon</m> for all <m>n \geq N</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-23-3">
    <title>The Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass Theorem. Read Zorn's
        proof and note which strategy it uses (for example, the
        monotone-subsequence approach or bisection). Whatever Zorn's
        choice, answer the questions below about the
        monotone-subsequence strategy.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            In the monotone-subsequence strategy, the first step is
            to show every sequence of reals has a monotone subsequence.
            Why is that claim not obvious?
          </li>
          <li>
            Once a monotone subsequence has been produced, how do the
            hypotheses of the theorem ensure that the subsequence is
            bounded?
          </li>
          <li>
            How does Monotone Convergence finish the argument?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-23-4">
    <title>Applying Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the bounded sequence
        <m>a_n = (-1)^n \cdot \dfrac{n}{n+1}</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Verify that <m>(a_n)</m> is bounded.
          </li>
          <li>
            Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass guarantees a convergent
            subsequence. Identify one such subsequence explicitly and
            compute its limit (using limit laws, not
            <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m>).
          </li>
          <li>
            Find a second convergent subsequence with a different
            limit. What does this show about <m>(a_n)</m> itself?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>|a_n| = n/(n+1) \lt 1</m>, so <m>|a_n| \leq 1</m> for all
        <m>n</m>; the sequence is bounded.
        (b) The even subsequence <m>a_{2k} = 2k/(2k+1) \to 1</m>
        (limit laws: divide by <m>2k</m>).
        (c) The odd subsequence <m>a_{2k-1} = -(2k-1)/(2k) \to -1</m>.
        Two subsequences with different limits implies <m>(a_n)</m>
        itself diverges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 2.4: CAUCHY SEQUENCES
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-sg-24-intro">
    <title>Section 2.4: Cauchy Sequences (Zorn pp. 114<ndash/>120)</title>
    <p>
      Section 2.4 reframes convergence without reference to a limit.
      Read Zorn's Definition 2.17 and Theorem 2.20 carefully. The equivalence
      of <q>convergent</q> and <q>Cauchy</q> in <m>\R</m> is another
      face of completeness: in <m>\Q</m> it fails, because a Cauchy
      sequence of rationals need not converge in <m>\Q</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-24-1">
    <title>The Cauchy Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State Zorn's definition of a Cauchy sequence symbolically.
        Identify every quantifier and its type.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare to the definition of convergence. Which <em>variable</em>
        appears in the convergence definition but is <em>absent</em>
        from the Cauchy definition? What is the conceptual significance
        of that absence?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0\ \exists N \in \N\ \forall m, n \geq N:
        |a_m - a_n| \lt \varepsilon</m>. The absent variable is the
        limit <m>L</m>: the Cauchy definition talks only about the
        terms of the sequence among themselves. Conceptually, this is
        what makes Cauchy a <q>purely internal</q> notion of convergence,
        definable without reference to an external candidate limit.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-24-2">
    <title>Convergent Implies Cauchy</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's proof that every convergent sequence is Cauchy.
        The core identity is
        <md>a_m - a_n = (a_m - L) + (L - a_n).</md>
        Explain how this identity, combined with the Triangle Inequality
        and the <m>\varepsilon/2</m>-split, delivers the Cauchy
        conclusion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Is the Completeness Axiom used in this direction of the proof?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        By the Triangle Inequality applied to the identity,
        <m>|a_m - a_n| \leq |a_m - L| + |L - a_n|</m>. If
        <m>a_n \to L</m>, pick <m>N</m> so that <m>|a_k - L| \lt \varepsilon/2</m>
        for <m>k \geq N</m>; then for <m>m, n \geq N</m>,
        <m>|a_m - a_n| \lt \varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon</m>.
        Completeness is <em>not</em> used in this direction: the
        existence of <m>L</m> is given by hypothesis.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-sg-24-3">
    <title>Cauchy Implies Convergent (where completeness enters)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's proof that in <m>\R</m>, every Cauchy sequence
        converges. The argument has three steps:
      </p>
      <p>
        Step 1: A Cauchy sequence is bounded.
        Step 2: By Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass, a bounded sequence has
        a convergent subsequence.
        Step 3: A Cauchy sequence with a convergent subsequence converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each step, identify: what theorem or property of <m>\R</m>
        does it use, and is completeness needed?
      </p>
      <p>
        Conclude: In <m>\Q</m>, Cauchy sequences need not converge.
        Give one familiar example.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Step 1: direct argument using the Cauchy property with
        <m>\varepsilon = 1</m>; no completeness needed.
        Step 2: Bolzano<ndash/>Weierstrass, which does use completeness
        (either through the Monotone Convergence Theorem (MCT) or through a nested-intervals argument).
        Step 3: Triangle-inequality argument; no additional completeness.
        Thus completeness enters via Step 2.
        In <m>\Q</m>: any Cauchy sequence of rationals converging to
        <m>\sqrt{2}</m> in <m>\R</m>, e.g., the decimal truncations
        <m>1, 1.4, 1.41, 1.414, \ldots</m>, is Cauchy in <m>\Q</m>
        but has no limit in <m>\Q</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

</section>
